{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-105630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/5e99e02134a5f42aa440cd2d83c971afee3cdb3c210b4488ebccf5cfff1ef8ba.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-3,0]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,0],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(0)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-3)=-1$ and $f(0)=1$, we must have \n$k\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$-1$ and $1$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/50f937db1a3a87b1887eb22851b285190f1a8999c986af7f1cf32a1676873da2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-105630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ and $0$"] ["text" "$-1$ and $1$"] ["text" "$-3$ and $0$"] ["text" "$-3$ and $1$"] ["text" "$0$ and $1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-1$ and $1$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III, listed below, be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\n- continuous\n- $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively\n- defined" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II"] ["text" "I, III"] ["text" "II, I"] ["text" "II, III"] ["text" "III, II"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+x$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[1,8].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [1,8]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,8],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(8).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(8)=10$, we must have \n$k\\in [2,10].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$2$ and $10$\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-2$ and $4$"] ["text" "$-4$ and $0$"] ["text" "$1$ and $2$"] ["text" "$2$ and $10$"] ["text" "$2$ and $8$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2$ and $10$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/2dd5fb6cdd180dffc3f4ad5c1742b0fc60ecaf31e5ad6c299c101c44a7f6654b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-2)=6$ and $f(3)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [2,6].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$2$ and $6$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/567a6a601c80d1af5adcb10188d6ae4de83aa154505d59945cadbc226f05eb4b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$0$ and $6$"] ["text" "$1$ and $5$"] ["text" "$1$ and $6$"] ["text" "$2$ and $5$"] ["text" "$2$ and $6$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2$ and $6$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4906a1de7cc1eb78bbff845fe6b03b024b47c46e84c801609e8da40a5d37c864.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $2$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(2)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(2)=2.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,2]$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/455863da8f126505f018a356f5f89bbbea81f1e1e90e803a51390bed22514a9a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "c\\in (-2,2)"] ["math" "c\\in (1,2)"] ["math" "c\\in [-2,2)"] ["math" "c\\in [-2,2]"] ["math" "c\\in [1,2]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-48361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "c\\in [-2,2]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-4"] ["math" "k=-5"] ["math" "k=10"] ["math" "k=12"] ["math" "k=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[1,4]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(4)=66,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,66].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[2,66].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=1"] ["math" "k=10"] ["math" "k=5"] ["math" "k=50"] ["math" "k=65"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=8-(x+2)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-2,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=8$ and $f(1)=-1,$ we must have \n$k\\in 