e value theorem **not** guarantee that $g(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[2,4]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4]$, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $g(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $g(2)$ and $g(4).$\n\nSince $g(2)=0$ and $g(4)=-12,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-12,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-12,0].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-1"] ["math" "k=-6"] ["math" "k=-8"] ["math" "k=0"] ["math" "k=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-3,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-3,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-3)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-3)=-7$ and $f(1)=1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-7,1].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=-2$ belongs to the interval $[-7,1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/3a9cf79e319e3a4a8d7d5195d65bc01e69bceb3721c29ca905132c1a607ba413.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "k=-2"] ["math" "k=-9"] ["math" "k=2"] ["math" "k=3"] ["math" "k=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "k=-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $f(2)=3$ and $f(3)=1.$ Since $2\\in [1,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,6],$ we have $f(3)=1$ and $f(6)=4.$ Since $2\\in [1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $f(6)=4$ and $f(7)=0.$ Since $2\\in [0,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=2$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,6]$ and $[6,7].$  Therefore, $f(x)=2$ has at least three solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-78537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [2,7].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $g(2)=-2$ and $g(3)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-2,1],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=1$ and $g(4)=-3.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $g(4)=-3$ and $g(5)=1.$ Since $-1\\in [-3,1],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,6],$ we have $g(5)=1$ and $g(6)=0.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $g(6)=0$ and $g(7)=2.$ Since $-1\\notin [0,2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-1$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,4]$  and $[4,5].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-1$ has at least three solutions on $[2,7].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-344761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $p(x)=2?$\n\nThe equation $p(x)=2$ has at least {{field-1}} solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-344761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-344761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-344761" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-344761" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to this function?\n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $[-1,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n\n- $[-4,-1]$\n- $[3,5]$\n- $[-1,4]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,3]"] ["math" "[-2,0]"] ["math" "[-2,1)"] ["math" "[-2,1]"] ["math" "[0,3]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[-2,0]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x(x+3)(x-2).$ On which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=-5$ has a solution?\n\n- $[-5,-3]$\n- $[-3,-2]$\n- $[-2,0]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it's continuous over the real numbers. Thus, we can apply the intermediate value theorem to any of the given intervals.\n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-70$ | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-5,-3],$ we have $f(-5)=-70$ and $f(-3)=0.$ Since $-5\\in [-70,0],$ the IVT does guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-3,-2],$ we have $f(-3)=0$ and $f(-2)=8.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[-2,0],$ we have $f(-2)=8$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-5\\notin [0,8],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-5$ on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-47035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+5x+6}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,3]$ is allowed. The intervals $[-8,-3)$ and $(-2,1]$ are not closed, while $[-5,-3]$ and $[-4,-2]$ contain the point $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28ce177bfa929a5da0e2e2c0faee184f76b99bf18c3f2eb27e4a10b954f09c5f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,1]"] ["math" "[-1,3]"] ["math" "[-4,-2]"] ["math" "[-5,-3]"] ["math" "[-8,-3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "q-105710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[-1,3]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/state.json" "file_sha256" "47a1fd7542821ae8986b639dcfbef600897446ad4305515a3916c957ff82936f"} {"question" "e-6753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-6753.json" "file_sha256" "d1aaa0679b986141927dfd32779effd497a030ba2d7ef53bb4453abb6ad8a427"} {"question" "e-6753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-6753.json" "file_sha256" "d1aaa0679b986141927dfd32779effd497a030ba2d7ef53bb4453abb6ad8a427"} {"question" "e-114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-114.json" "file_sha256" "bcdc7c22426c2d173e2fdc703ef05b885fb8c0a573deb69d593ac5b994f203f8"} {"question" "e-114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-114.json" "file_sha256" "bcdc7c22426c2d173e2fdc703ef05b885fb8c0a573deb69d593ac5b994f203f8"} {"question" "e-115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-115.json" "file_sha256" "fa3dbc1e7856ad7f59e6909f4a5b721b5a98c0f0ee1b57e3daa82082e64e43eb"} {"question" "e-115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-115.json" "file_sha256" "fa3dbc1e7856ad7f59e6909f4a5b721b5a98c0f0ee1b57e3daa82082e64e43eb"} {"question" "e-116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-116.json" "file_sha256" "312f239f2bf1df6acdaa6c4d3f53f95a53cc11880d5afbb8f4fc58211ba65471"} {"question" "e-116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/example-116.json" "file_sha256" "312f239f2bf1df6acdaa6c4d3f53f95a53cc11880d5afbb8f4fc58211ba65471"}] "usage" {"e-115" [] "e-116" [] "q-47058" [] "e-6753" [] "e-114" []} "basis" 2032 "retractions" [[17592186134395 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = (x + 1)^{2} - 4$. For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x) = k$ has a solution on the interval $x ∈ [- 4,-2]$?"] [17592186133570 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2]$. By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/6e256c36411942eac5fc5523f999c4c3.png)\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{________}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*"] [17592186133570 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-5)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(2)=4\n$$\n\n, we must have $k\\in[1,4]$.\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n> *If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k$.*"] [17592186133398 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2$. According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]$?"] [17592186133398 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2150/Source/Images/0d850b5400cf74888676e94e8c489eff.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6]$, so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nhas a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6)$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(6)=11\n$$\n\n, we must have\n$k\\in[3,11]$.\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function,\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nmay have solutions for $k < 3$, but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$."] [17592186133399 :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2]$. Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2$?"] [17592186133399 :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2]$.\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$. Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}]$, we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}$. Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}]$, the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$. Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions."] [17592186133402 :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\,$?"] [17592186133402 :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0$, which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1$.\n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132813 17592186132814 17592186132815 17592186132816 17592186132817] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132812 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132812 :answer-field/id] [17592186132812 :answer-field/key] [17592186132812 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
